*======================================================================
* vmmem.a99 - the story image in SAMS, seen through three 4K windows.
*
* The image lives in SAMS from page STPAGE; story byte address a is in
* story page a/4096 at offset a mod 4096.  Three windows look at it:
*
*   A  >E000  the code being run.  R4 = PC (an address in the window),
*             R5 = its story page.  FB moves to the next page when R4
*             reaches >F000.
*   B  >F000  data: the object table, properties, globals, arrays,
*             dictionary and substitutions, all in the first 64K, so a
*             16-bit story address names them.  CURBH holds the base
*             address (>p000) of the page mapped there; >FFFF = none.
*   D  >D000  text being printed: a string can be anywhere in 256K.
*
* A word never straddles two pages: words are on even addresses and
* pages start on multiples of 4096.  Strings and routines may run over
* a page end; the readers follow them.
*
* Register rules for these helpers: R0 returns the value; R2, R3, R12
* are clobbered; R9 and R13 are link registers as each routine says.
*======================================================================

* MAPWIN - map story page R2 into the window whose mapper register is at
* R3 (MAPRGA, MAPRGB or MAPRGD).  Clobbers R2, R12.  Leaf.
* The card takes the page in either byte, so the page goes in both:
* MOVB @WS+5 copies R2's low byte into its high byte.
MAPWIN AI   R2,STPAGE
       MOVB @WS+5,R2
       LI   R12,AMSCRU
       SBO  0
       MOV  R2,*R3
       SBZ  0
       RT

* SETPC - set the PC to story address (page R5, offset R1 0-4095) and
* map the page into window A.  Clobbers R1-R3, R12.  Leaf (tail call).
SETPC  MOV  R1,R4
       AI   R4,WINA
       MOV  R5,R2
       LI   R3,MAPRGA
       B    @MAPWIN

* PACKPC - set the PC from a packed address R1 (byte address R1*4):
* page = R1 / 1024, offset = (R1 mod 1024) * 4.  Clobbers R1-R3, R12.
PACKPC MOV  R1,R5
       SRL  R5,10
       ANDI R1,>03FF
       SLA  R1,2
       JMP  SETPC

* FB, FW, RDW, WRW, RDB, WRB and the dispatch loop NEXT are in fast.a99:
* they run from the scratchpad.  These are their slow paths.

* FBWS - FB's slow path: the byte fetched was a page's last; move the
* PC to the next page's start.  Returns to FB's caller.
FBWS   INC  R5
       LI   R4,WINA
       MOV  R5,R2
       LI   R3,MAPRGA
       B    @MAPWIN

* FWSLOW - FW's slow path, near a page end: two byte fetches.
FWSLOW MOV  R11,R13
       BL   @FB
       MOV  R0,R1
       SWPB R1
       BL   @FB
       SOC  R0,R1
       B    *R13

* BREMAP - window B onto the page whose base (a multiple of >1000) is in
* R2; CURBH remembers it.  Clobbers R2, R12 (MAPWIN).  Leaf (tail call).
BREMAP MOV  R2,@CURBH
       SRL  R2,12
       LI   R3,MAPRGB
       B    @MAPWIN

* TXSET - start reading the string at packed address R1, in window D.
* Clobbers R1-R3, R12.  Leaf.
TXSET  MOV  R1,R2
       SRL  R2,10              page
       MOV  R2,@TPAGE
       ANDI R1,>03FF
       SLA  R1,2
       AI   R1,WIND
       MOV  R1,@TPTR
       LI   R3,MAPRGD
       B    @MAPWIN

* TXNEXT - the next byte of the string (R0, 0-255).  Window D must still
* show TPAGE: anything that maps window D in between must call TXREMAP.
* Clobbers R0-R3, R12.  Leaf.
TXNEXT MOV  @TPTR,R1
       MOVB *R1+,R0
       SRL  R0,8
       CI   R1,WIND+>1000
       JL   TXN1
       INC  @TPAGE             the string runs into the next page
       LI   R1,WIND
       MOV  R1,@TPTR
       MOV  @TPAGE,R2
       LI   R3,MAPRGD
       B    @MAPWIN
TXN1   MOV  R1,@TPTR
       RT
